/* VaCuus M2 interaction demo - stylesheet.

   RENDERER SUBSET, same as m1_hud.rcss and for the same reason: the M1 recorder
   implements solid fills (including alpha), 1px borders, border-radius as plain
   geometry, text, <img>, 2D transforms, and scissor clipping via a non-visible
   `overflow`. NO decorators/gradients, NO box-shadow, NO filter or
   backdrop-filter, NO transitions or animations (M5+).

   ONE HARD RULE FOLLOWS FROM THAT: #list-clip below must have NEITHER
   border-radius NOR transform. Either would push RmlUi off the scissor path onto
   the clip-mask path, which this recorder does not implement, and the list would
   render unclipped over the rest of the screen. */

/* RmlUi ships NO user-agent stylesheet -- verified rather than repeated: there is
   not one .rcss file anywhere in Source/ThirdParty/RmlUi, and `display` is
   registered with a default of "inline"
   (ThirdParty/RmlUi/Source/Core/StyleSheetSpecification.cpp:306). So every tag
   that needs a box has to be told, including the scrollbar internals further
   down. */
div { display: block; }
span { display: inline; }

body
{
	display: block;
	width: 100%;
	height: 100%;
	font-family: LatoLatin;
	font-size: 16px;
	color: #D2E9F2;

	/* THE NAVIGATION BOOTSTRAP, and it is load-bearing twice over.

	   <body> IS the document element in RML (Factory registers the `body` tag to
	   the ElementDocument instancer, ThirdParty/RmlUi/Source/Core/Factory.cpp:180),
	   and AdoptDocument shows the document with FocusFlag::Document -- so at load
	   the document element itself holds focus.

	   The arrow-key branch of ElementDocument::ProcessDefaultAction then resolves
	   `focus_node` to the nearest focusable ancestor of the focus leaf and FALLS
	   BACK TO THE DOCUMENT when there is none (ElementDocument.cpp:618-626), and
	   reads the nav property off THAT element with GetLocalProperty
	   (ElementDocument.cpp:628). With no local `nav-*` on the document the lookup
	   returns null and the whole branch is skipped: the first direction key is a
	   silent no-op and the UI looks dead to a pad. With it,
	   FindNextNavigationElement short-cuts `current_element == this` to tab order
	   (ElementDocument.cpp:798-799) and the press enters the grid.

	   (The brief and several M2 comments cite 795-796 for that short-cut; at the
	   vendored SHA 0ae381e it is 798-799 -- :795 is the Nav::TreeOrder case.)

	   `nav` is a Box SHORTHAND over nav-up/right/down/left
	   (StyleSheetSpecification.cpp:382), so one value sets all four. */
	nav: auto;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Spatial navigation and focusability, per element                    */
/*                                                                     */
/* PER ELEMENT BY NECESSITY. All four nav-* properties are registered   */
/* with inherited=false (StyleSheetSpecification.cpp:378-381) and are   */
/* read with GetLocalProperty (ElementDocument.cpp:628), which sees     */
/* only inline styles and the element's OWN matched definition. The     */
/* `body { nav: auto }` above therefore does nothing for anything       */
/* inside body.                                                        */
/*                                                                     */
/* `tab-index: auto` comes along because it is RmlUi's focusability     */
/* requirement (CanFocusElement, ElementDocument.cpp:30-43) -- without  */
/* it an element is skipped by Tab, by the arrows and by the pad, and   */
/* Return/Space will not click it either (ElementDocument.cpp:641-650   */
/* gates Click() on the focus leaf's computed tab-index).              */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
button, input
{
	nav: auto;
	tab-index: auto;
}

#m2-title
{
	position: absolute;
	left: 40px;
	top: 22px;
	font-size: 19px;
	color: #7DE6FF;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Panels. Plain divs: no tab-index, no marker attribute, so the        */
/* interactive predicate does NOT report them.                          */
/*                                                                     */
/* THAT IS NOT COSMETIC. A reported panel rect would cover its own      */
/* children, and because pass-through is the absence of coverage rather */
/* than an occluder (FVaCuusInteractiveSnapshot's class comment), an    */
/* interactive #p-pass would take the very clicks #pass-zone exists to  */
/* let through. Keeping the panels inert is what makes section 4 work.  */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
#p-buttons, #p-list, #p-input, #p-pass
{
	position: absolute;
	width: 420px;
	padding: 10px;
	background-color: #10202AE6;
	border: 1px #3A5A6C;
	border-radius: 8px;
}
#p-buttons { left: 40px;  top: 60px; }
#p-list    { left: 40px;  top: 250px; }
#p-input   { left: 500px; top: 60px; }
#p-pass    { left: 500px; top: 250px; }

.panel-title
{
	margin-bottom: 10px;
	font-size: 12px;
	color: #8FA9B5;
}

.hint
{
	margin-top: 8px;
	font-size: 11px;
	color: #6F8894;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 1. Buttons                                                          */
/*                                                                     */
/* Three unmistakably different states, because a screenshot is the     */
/* evidence: dark slate -> amber on :hover -> orange on :active ->      */
/* magenta on :focus. Magenta for focus specifically so a pad-driven    */
/* screenshot with no cursor anywhere near the button cannot be read as */
/* a hover.                                                            */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
.m2-btn
{
	display: inline-block;
	width: 120px;
	height: 44px;
	margin-right: 8px;
	line-height: 42px;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 15px;
	color: #9FDCEF;
	background-color: #0D1B22;
	border: 1px #2F5566;
	border-radius: 6px;

	/* Latched by FVaCuusSystemInterface::SetMouseCursor and published in the
	   snapshot, which is what SVaCuusWidget::OnCursorQuery answers Slate from. */
	cursor: pointer;
}
.m2-btn.last { margin-right: 0; }

.m2-btn:hover
{
	color: #05090C;
	background-color: #FFC44D;
	border-color: #FFE9AE;
}

/* :active is set by RmlUi on mouse-DOWN, for the primary button only and only
   while the point is inside the element (Element.cpp:2018-2023). It is also set
   on every ancestor in the bubble chain that contains the point -- which is why
   no panel here styles :active. */
.m2-btn:active
{
	color: #FFFFFF;
	background-color: #FF6A2A;
	border-color: #FFC44D;
}

.m2-btn:focus
{
	color: #FFFFFF;
	background-color: #7A2A8F;
	border-color: #FF6AF0;
}

/* An explicit nav edge, to exercise the string form of the property: from the
   last button, "right" leaves the row and goes to the text field instead of
   wrapping. The leading '#' is REQUIRED -- without it RmlUi logs a warning and
   navigates nowhere (ElementDocument.cpp:760-766) -- and the quotes are required
   too, because the value is parsed by the "string" parser after the keyword
   parser rejects it (StyleSheetSpecification.cpp:378-381). */
#btn-c { nav-right: "#m2-input"; }

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 2. Wheel-scrollable list                                            */
/*                                                                     */
/* THREE THINGS ARE ALL REQUIRED for the wheel to move anything, and    */
/* dropping any one of them fails silently:                             */
/*                                                                     */
/*  - display: block. An `inline` element is routed to FormatInlineBox  */
/*    and never becomes a ContainerBox, so it can never be a scroll     */
/*    container -- and RmlUi does NOT blockify on `overflow`.           */
/*  - an explicit height. With height:auto the available space degrades */
/*    to HUGE_VALF (Layout/ContainerBox.cpp:137-141), the box grows to  */
/*    fit, and GetScrollHeight() > GetClientHeight() stays false        */
/*    (Element.cpp:2005-2006) -- so GetClosestScrollableContainer       */
/*    returns the parent and the wheel scrolls nothing.                 */
/*  - overflow-y: auto or scroll. Those are the only two values         */
/*    Element::GetClosestScrollableContainer accepts                    */
/*    (Element.cpp:2002-2003).                                          */
/*                                                                     */
/* `scroll` rather than `auto` so the bar exists unconditionally         */
/* (Layout/ContainerBox.cpp:17,22) and is therefore always draggable in */
/* a screenshot; overflow-x stays hidden so a long row cannot add a      */
/* second bar and change the layout under the test.                     */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
#list-clip
{
	display: block;
	height: 176px;
	overflow-y: scroll;
	overflow-x: hidden;
	background-color: #0A141AC8;
	border: 1px #223844;
	/* NO border-radius and NO transform here -- see the file header. */
}

.row
{
	height: 24px;
	padding: 0 8px;
	font-size: 13px;
	line-height: 24px;
	color: #C4DEE9;
}
.row.alt { background-color: #14222CB4; }

/* The scrollbar internals. RmlUi creates them as non-DOM children under the tags
   `scrollbarvertical` (ElementScroll.cpp:193) and `slidertrack` / `sliderbar` /
   `sliderarrowdec` / `sliderarrowinc` (WidgetScroll.cpp:84-87), and with no
   user-agent stylesheet they are invisible and zero-sized until styled here.

   The vertical scrollbar's WIDTH is what ElementScroll measures to reserve space
   (ElementScroll.cpp:43-44), so it has to be set. The bar's LENGTH is computed
   from the scroll ratio while its height is auto, with min-height honoured
   (WidgetScroll.cpp:295-301) -- hence min-height rather than height, or a
   twenty-row list would give a bar too small to grab.

   The two arrows are deliberately left unsized: WidgetScroll clamps a negative
   box to (0,0) (WidgetScroll.cpp:215-218), so no rule means no arrows. */
scrollbarvertical
{
	display: block;
	width: 14px;
	background-color: #071016;
}
slidertrack
{
	display: block;
	background-color: #0C1A21;
}
sliderbar
{
	display: block;
	width: 14px;
	min-height: 28px;
	background-color: #2F5566;
	border-radius: 3px;
}
sliderbar:hover { background-color: #4E869D; }
sliderbar:active { background-color: #7DE6FF; }

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 3. Text input                                                       */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* `cursor: text` is not decoration: RmlUi pushes the name through
   SystemInterface::SetMouseCursor, VaCuus latches it into the published snapshot
   and SVaCuusWidget::OnCursorQuery answers Slate from it -- so an I-beam here and
   an arrow over the buttons is evidence that the geometry deciding
   EVaCuusRectFlags::TextInput is the same geometry the cursor came from. */
#m2-input
{
	display: block;
	width: 380px;
	height: 34px;
	padding: 0px 8px;
	font-size: 15px;
	color: #DCF2FA;
	background-color: #0A1419;
	border: 1px #2F5566;
	border-radius: 6px;
	cursor: text;
}
/* Styled differently from .m2-btn:focus on purpose: a screenshot has to say which
   control holds the caret, and "the border went cyan" is readable at 1920x1080
   where a one-pixel caret is not. */
#m2-input:focus
{
	background-color: #0E2430;
	border-color: #5FE0FF;
}
/* RmlUi's own selection element, for the IME composition span. Unreachable on
   Linux (no ITextInputMethodSystem, so no composition ever starts) and styled
   anyway, because it is where a Windows validation pass would look. */
#m2-input selection
{
	color: #05090C;
	background-color: #5FE0FF;
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* 4. Pass-through region                                              */
/*                                                                     */
/* `tab-index: auto` is what makes this zone interactive AND focusable  */
/* by the snapshot's own predicate, so the `vacuus-passthrough`         */
/* attribute in the RML is the ONLY reason it is not reported. Remove   */
/* the attribute and clicks here stop reaching the game -- that is the  */
/* before/after this section exists to make available.                  */
/*                                                                     */
/* Deliberately NOT styled with :focus/:hover: the zone never receives  */
/* either, because it is never reported and therefore never hit -- and  */
/* a rule that could not fire would be a lie in a stylesheet.          */
/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
#pass-zone
{
	display: block;
	height: 130px;
	padding: 10px;
	tab-index: auto;
	background-color: #1A0F1EB4;
	border: 1px #6B4A7A;
	border-radius: 6px;
}
#pass-label
{
	font-size: 12px;
	color: #C79FD8;
}
#pass-inner
{
	display: block;
	width: 200px;
	height: 34px;
	margin-top: 12px;
	line-height: 32px;
	text-align: center;
	font-size: 12px;
	color: #C79FD8;
	background-color: #241429;
	border: 1px #6B4A7A;
	border-radius: 4px;
}
